## Phase 5: Importing with the `import` block (Terraform 1.5+)

Finally, I wanted to test the modern, code-driven way of importing.

1. I added an S3 Public Access Block declaration inside `resou.tf`:
```hcl
resource "aws_s3_bucket_public_access_block" "own_mport_s3_bucket_for_pub_usage" {
  bucket = aws_s3_bucket.own_mport_s3.bucket

  block_public_acls       = true
  block_public_policy     = true
  ignore_public_acls      = true
  restrict_public_buckets = true
}

```


2. I created `own_import.tf` containing the `import` block:
```hcl
import {
  to = aws_s3_bucket_public_access_block.own_mport_s3_bucket_for_pub_usage
  id = "my-car-bucket-2645"
}

```


3. I ran `terraform fmt` to standardize formatting, followed by `terraform plan`.

Instead of an error, Terraform automatically detected the block and generated an import plan:

```text
Plan: 1 to import, 0 to add, 0 to change, 0 to destroy.

```

I applied the change:

```bash
terraform apply --auto-approve

```

```text
aws_s3_bucket_public_access_block.own_mport_s3_bucket_for_pub_usage: Importing... [id=my-car-bucket-2645]
